completion:
The first philosopher I know of was a man who proposed that we should live in complete harmony with ourselves. He said he had made an experiment of this kind for himself; and if his experience were true it must be so much more true than anything I have ever seen. If what is called my nature really exists, I am quite certain that it cannot exist without other things too. There are many things that I do which I do not really understand. I seem always to get into a state where I can think myself out of them by making them less like themselves.
 So far I agree perfectly with him; but then he went on: “There is no room in the world for these two things.” What! No room for a whole country? He seemed to mean, there must be something else besides himself and the rest of us. This does not answer. For example, there are some things that are very necessary to me, and yet they are necessary only because I make them necessary; namely, bread and clothes.
 But I suppose that if he came back again and explained that he was talking metaphorically, I could perhaps see the point, though I still wonder whether he meant metaphorically, or whether he believed it literally. Perhaps I ought to say that I have often thought that it might be easier to live harmoniously with one’s self than with oneself. For instance, if one wanted to eat breakfast and go down to work at 8 o’clock in the morning, one did not want to eat breakfast and go down to work at 7 o'clock. But the idea seems strange enough in itself. When men talk about living in harmony with the universe, I suppose they always mean living in harmony with themselves. And when they talk about being united with God, I suppose they never mean being united with themselves. But when they talk of uniting with oneself, it seems queerer yet. It is hard indeed to unite with oneself. You cannot even be in love with yourself;
